\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Definition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A family of non-empty sets of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

, satisfies the pair-and-triple condition if it contains, at least, all
 subsets of two and three elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Claim:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the pair-and-triple condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Proof:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the pair-and-triple condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies\forall\ \{x_{1},x_{2}\}\subseteq X\ \wedge\ \forall\ \{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\}\subseteq X:\left\{ \{x_{1},x_{2}\},\{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\}\right\} \subseteq\mathcal{B}^{W}\equiv\{B(p,w):p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Without loss of generality, take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{1},x_{2}\}\in\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies B(p,w)=\{x_{1},x_{2}\}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)\equiv\{x\in X:p\cdot x\le w\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{1}\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{2}\leq w$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z=\frac{1}{2}(x_{1}+x_{2})\in X$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{1}\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{2}\leq w$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot z\leq w$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies z\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But this contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x_{1},x_{2}\}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the pair-and-triple condition
\end_layout

